WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
In 1982, human insulin made by bacteria was

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.680
approved, and it's now used around the world.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.920
In 2018, a researcher edited a gene in

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.320
human embryos, hoping twin girls would resist HIV.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.360
Scientists everywhere condemned it, and he was sent

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.280
to prison.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.600
Both used genetic technology, and both promised a

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.800
health benefit.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.120
So that label alone can't tell you which

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.480
one to trust.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.200
Which benefits actually hold up, and how can

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:29.840
you tell?

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:33.546
A benefit is never just good in general.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.906
It's better than something, shown by evidence, for

00:00:36.987 --> 00:00:37.546
someone.

00:00:38.107 --> 00:00:39.546
So ask three questions.

00:00:40.027 --> 00:00:40.826
Better than what?

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:42.186
Shown how?

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:43.466
Who gets it?

00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:45.626
Try them on the embryo edit.

00:00:46.187 --> 00:00:49.066
The father had HIV, but washing his sperm

00:00:49.147 --> 00:00:52.906
before IVF already protects an embryo, and HIV

00:00:52.987 --> 00:00:54.906
can be prevented in other ways later in

00:00:55.107 --> 00:00:55.626
life.

00:00:55.947 --> 00:00:58.586
The edits didn't copy the natural protective version

00:00:58.627 --> 00:01:01.466
of the gene, and their effects were untested.

00:01:01.507 --> 00:01:03.786
And the twins carried all the risk, with

00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:04.986
no say.

00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:06.586
It fails all three.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.160
Start on the farm, where benefits include harvests,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.000
pest resistance and nutrition.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.680
In the nineteen-nineties, a virus was killing papaya

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.040
trees across Hawaii's main growing district, and no

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.800
ordinary variety resisted it.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.680
Scientists gave papaya a copy of one of

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.200
the virus's genes.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.840
The plant's cells use it to recognise the

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.680
virus's genetic material and destroy it.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:32.880
Better than what?

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.400
Trees that were dying.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.320
Shown how?

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.600
The district's harvest had roughly halved, and after

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.040
growers got the seed in 1998, harvests climbed

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:42.640
back.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.720
Who gets it?

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:44.640
The growers.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.920
That benefit holds up strongly.

00:01:48.134 --> 00:01:49.253
Now medicine.

00:01:49.494 --> 00:01:51.533
Bacteria make human insulin.

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:52.533
Better than what?

00:01:52.934 --> 00:01:55.493
Insulin from pig and cattle pancreases.

00:01:55.974 --> 00:01:58.533
Pig insulin differs from ours by one amino

00:01:58.614 --> 00:02:01.013
acid, and cattle insulin by three.

00:02:01.494 --> 00:02:03.733
You'd expect the human version to work much

00:02:03.814 --> 00:02:04.413
better.

00:02:04.694 --> 00:02:07.813
But when trials compared them, purified pig insulin

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:10.213
controlled blood sugar about as well for most

00:02:10.294 --> 00:02:10.853
patients.

00:02:11.414 --> 00:02:13.333
So the benefit that holds up isn't a

00:02:13.414 --> 00:02:14.373
better medicine.

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:15.573
It's the supply.

00:02:16.054 --> 00:02:19.653
Insulin no longer depends on pancreases from abattoirs,

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:22.813
and fermenters make a consistent product at scale.

00:02:23.094 --> 00:02:23.973
Who gets it?

00:02:24.214 --> 00:02:25.693
That depends on price.

00:02:25.974 --> 00:02:29.253
Many people with diabetes worldwide still can't reliably

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:29.813
get it.

00:02:31.034 --> 00:02:33.993
Now compare growth hormone, given to children whose

00:02:34.034 --> 00:02:35.353
bodies don't make enough.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:39.193
Until 1985, it came from the pituitary glands

00:02:39.274 --> 00:02:40.793
of people who had died.

00:02:41.034 --> 00:02:43.833
Supply was scarce, and some batches carried a

00:02:43.994 --> 00:02:45.673
fatal brain disease.

00:02:45.794 --> 00:02:48.233
That year it was withdrawn, and hormone made

00:02:48.274 --> 00:02:50.393
by bacteria replaced it.

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:53.193
The old source was scarce and dangerous, so

00:02:53.274 --> 00:02:55.353
the benefit is far bigger.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:57.273
Better than what sets how much a benefit

00:02:57.354 --> 00:02:58.073
is worth.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.220
Industry follows the same logic.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.180
Once a microbe makes a useful protein, it

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.060
can make it at factory scale.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.140
Take chymosin, the enzyme that curdles milk for

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:09.780
cheese.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.420
It used to come from calves' stomachs.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:13.460
Better than what?

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.500
Microbes now make the same enzyme, so the

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.060
cheese turns out the same, and supply doesn't

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.180
depend on calves.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:21.340
Shown how?

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.260
Since about 1990, it's been used for much

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.380
of the world's cheese.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:26.500
Who gets it?

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.940
Cheesemakers, with a steady supply at lower cost.

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:34.606
In medicine and industry, the engineered microbes usually

00:03:34.727 --> 00:03:37.646
stay sealed in tanks, and only the purified

00:03:37.847 --> 00:03:39.166
product leaves.

00:03:39.247 --> 00:03:42.766
On a farm, the modified organism is released.

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:45.726
It grows among wild species, its pests can

00:03:45.807 --> 00:03:48.766
evolve resistance, and if one variety is planted

00:03:48.847 --> 00:03:52.526
everywhere, the crop's genetic diversity narrows.

00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:55.726
That's why Bt cotton's benefit, far fewer sprays

00:03:55.807 --> 00:03:59.006
than ordinary cotton, holds up only while refuges

00:03:59.087 --> 00:04:00.846
keep resistance rare.

00:04:00.927 --> 00:04:03.006
A gain in harvest isn't a gain in

00:04:03.047 --> 00:04:04.046
biodiversity.

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:07.426
So which benefits hold up best?

00:04:07.907 --> 00:04:10.466
They're strongest where the technology did something the

00:04:10.547 --> 00:04:13.746
old method couldn't do safely, the evidence measures

00:04:13.827 --> 00:04:16.466
the real outcome, and the product reaches the

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:17.746
people who need it.

00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:20.466
Growth hormone and the papaya were far better

00:04:20.547 --> 00:04:22.946
than what came before, with clear evidence.

00:04:23.427 --> 00:04:26.866
Insulin and chymosin win clearly on supply, though

00:04:26.947 --> 00:04:29.186
insulin's reach depends on price.

00:04:29.507 --> 00:04:32.066
Other farm crops can bring big benefits, but

00:04:32.147 --> 00:04:34.386
the verdict depends on how they're managed, and

00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:35.266
who gains.

00:04:35.827 --> 00:04:38.386
So a benefit isn't measured by how advanced

00:04:38.427 --> 00:04:39.666
the technology is.

00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:43.106
Here's the test in three questions.

00:04:43.587 --> 00:04:44.466
Better than what?

00:04:45.027 --> 00:04:45.986
Shown how?

00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:47.266
Who gets it?

00:04:47.747 --> 00:04:50.306
And one reminder for the farm: a gain

00:04:50.387 --> 00:04:53.426
in harvest isn't a gain in biodiversity.

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:56.073
Two exam questions.

00:04:56.634 --> 00:04:57.393
First.

00:04:57.674 --> 00:05:01.113
A company makes laundry enzymes using genetically modified

00:05:01.194 --> 00:05:02.193
microbes.

00:05:02.474 --> 00:05:05.433
The enzymes remove stains at twenty degrees, where

00:05:05.514 --> 00:05:08.473
older detergents needed forty to sixty.

00:05:08.594 --> 00:05:10.713
Most of the energy a washing machine uses

00:05:10.794 --> 00:05:12.793
goes into heating the water.

00:05:12.874 --> 00:05:15.913
The company claims its enzymes cut household energy

00:05:15.994 --> 00:05:16.793
use.

00:05:16.914 --> 00:05:18.313
Assess this claim.

00:05:18.474 --> 00:05:19.753
Pause here to answer.

00:05:30.167 --> 00:05:32.086
The claim holds only partly.

00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:34.966
Compared with a hot wash, a twenty degree

00:05:35.047 --> 00:05:37.686
wash saves most of the energy, because heating

00:05:37.727 --> 00:05:39.206
the water uses the most.

00:05:39.687 --> 00:05:41.926
But the enzyme only makes a cold wash

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:42.646
possible.

00:05:43.127 --> 00:05:45.766
The saving happens only if people choose cold

00:05:45.807 --> 00:05:48.726
washes, so you'd want measured energy use in

00:05:48.807 --> 00:05:49.686
real homes.

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:52.726
Saying the enzyme saves energy by itself is

00:05:52.807 --> 00:05:54.086
the classic mistake.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:55.860
Second.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.260
The first hepatitis B vaccine, from 1981, used

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.220
a virus protein purified from the blood plasma

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.060
of people who carried the virus.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.900
From 1986, yeast carrying the gene for the

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.220
same protein made it in fermenters.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:13.900
Both vaccines protected well.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:17.460
Evaluate the benefit of the yeast-made vaccine compared

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.020
with the plasma vaccine.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:20.660
Pause here to answer.

00:06:31.067 --> 00:06:34.506
Protection wasn't the gain, because both protected well.

00:06:34.987 --> 00:06:36.586
The gain was supply.

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:39.626
It no longer depended on plasma from infected

00:06:39.707 --> 00:06:42.746
donors, it could be made consistently at large

00:06:42.827 --> 00:06:45.786
scale, and it eased fears of infection from

00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:46.666
blood products.

00:06:47.307 --> 00:06:50.426
That helped make vaccinating every baby practical, as

00:06:50.667 --> 00:06:51.386
Australia does.

00:06:52.027 --> 00:06:54.386
So the benefit is large, and it rests

00:06:54.427 --> 00:06:56.106
on supply and safety.

00:06:56.747 --> 00:06:59.706
Saying it protects better is the classic mistake.

00:07:00.934 --> 00:07:03.893
So to evaluate a benefit, name the domain

00:07:04.054 --> 00:07:05.733
and the exact benefit.

00:07:05.974 --> 00:07:09.493
Then ask: better than what, shown how, and

00:07:09.654 --> 00:07:10.373
who gets it?

00:07:10.934 --> 00:07:14.133
Weigh it against the trade-offs, especially biodiversity on

00:07:14.214 --> 00:07:16.533
farms, and finish with a verdict.

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:21.333
Next, practice questions that weigh benefits against biodiversity

00:07:21.414 --> 00:07:22.213
and ethics.

00:07:23.434 --> 00:07:24.873
Here are the key notes.

00:07:25.114 --> 00:07:27.033
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:27.114 --> 00:07:27.913
to copy them.
